    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain the physical relationship between water temperature and salinity in terms of their effect on seawater density? I'm looking at some AUV sensor data and need to ensure my understanding of the thermohaline drivers is solid.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Seawater density increases when both temperature decreases and salinity increases, as colder water contracts and salt adds mass per unit volume. Conversely, warmer water expands and becomes less dense, while fresher (lower salinity) water reduces density by removing dissolved particles. These combined changes drive thermohaline circulation, where deep, salty, cold water sinks and moves horizontally before eventually returning to the surface.",
      "source": "target",
