    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. I'm seeing some really weird spikes in the salinity readings at about 2000m depth in my dataset, though. It doesn't follow the expected gradient at all—it's almost like the sensor is just jumping around randomly for a few minutes before stabilizing. Any idea why I'd see such erratic, non-linear shifts in a stable environment like that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those erratic spikes at 2000m depth likely indicate transient sensor artifacts rather than a true physical change, as deep-sea gradients are typically stable against surface influences. Common causes include changes in ocean acoustic noise affecting CTD flow meter calibration or a momentary bubble interaction that alters the optical/scattering signal indoors. To confirm, cross-referencing those timestamps with the AUV's exact depth profile will show if the sensor drifted while the vehicle hovered at a constant pressure.",
      "source": "target",
